A broken-down truck can stall your entire business. Missed deadlines, frustrated customers, and unexpected repair bills pile up quickly when a vehicle just won’t stay fixed.

That is where the California Lemon Law for commercial vehicles comes in. Many business owners don’t realize these protections apply beyond personal cars. If your company relies on vans, service trucks, or a small fleet, the law may give you the right to a replacement or compensation.

And it’s more common than you might think. According to [estimates shared by KKTV](https://www.kktv.com/2023/09/27/lemon-laws-what-do-if-youve-been-sold-defective-car/), more than 150,000 new vehicles each year qualify as lemons.

In this article, we will explain California Lemon Law protections for commercial vehicles and fleets, what qualifies under the law, and how a California Lemon Law attorney can help protect your business.

Here’s what this guide will cover:

- An overview of California Lemon Law and who qualifies

- How the law applies specifically to commercial vehicles

- Key rules for small fleets and business operators

- Steps to take when filing a Lemon Law claim

## Overview of California Lemon Law

The [California Lemon Law](/info/how-it-works) is one of the strongest in the country. It protects buyers and lessees when a motor vehicle has a serious defect that can’t be fixed after repeated repair attempts.

While most people associate it with personal cars, the law also extends to small business use. If you run a company that depends on vans, light trucks, or small fleets, these protections may apply to you as well.

## Who Qualifies as a Lemon Vehicle Under California Lemon Law

Both individuals and small businesses can qualify. If you purchased or leased a vehicle in California under a manufacturer’s or dealer’s written warranty when the defect first appeared, you may be eligible.

This includes sole proprietors, partnerships, and corporations with a limited number of vehicles. Working with an experienced California Lemon Law attorney can help determine eligibility and build a strong claim.

## Types of Vehicles Covered

The lemon law for commercial vehicles covers new and used cars, pickup trucks, vans, and SUVs used primarily on public roads.

For business operators, the key factor is gross vehicle weight. Business vehicles under 10,000 pounds usually qualify. Heavy-duty trucks may not, and businesses with more than five registered vehicles in California are excluded.

## Timeframes and Mileage Limits

Defects must be reported while the vehicle is under its original or dealer warranty. There’s no strict mileage cap, but claims need to be filed before warranty coverage expires.

The law generally requires at least two unsuccessful repair attempts for a serious safety defect. Vehicles that spend 30 or more cumulative days out of service may also qualify under the lemon law for fleets and commercial use.

## Lemon Law for Commercial Vehicles

Commercial vehicles work harder than personal cars. They log more miles, carry heavier loads, and often spend long hours on the road.

When one of them develops a recurring defect, the financial hit can be immediate. The California Lemon Law for commercial vehicles recognizes these challenges but sets clear criteria to make sure claims are legitimate.

## Vehicle Weight and Usage Requirements

Coverage generally applies to light- and medium-duty vehicles under 10,000 pounds. That includes delivery vans, work trucks, and service vehicles that most small businesses rely on.

Heavier rigs, like large delivery trucks or specialty vehicles, typically fall outside the law’s protections, so checking weight specifications is an important first step.

## Differences for Business Versus Personal Vehicles

Unlike personal vehicles, business claims face an extra condition: the size of the fleet. The law protects companies with five or fewer vehicles registered in California.

Larger operations are excluded, making this remedy especially valuable for small businesses that can’t afford to lose a vehicle to endless repairs. Consulting an experienced Lemon Law attorney can clarify where your business falls.

## Common Commercial Vehicle Issues

The lemon law for fleets and commercial trucks and vehicles is meant for recurring problems that impair its use, value, or safety. Common issues include:

- Engine problems such as stalling, overheating, or oil leaks

- Transmission failures that disrupt performance or drivability

- Electrical malfunctions, including faulty wiring or repeated dead batteries

- Brake defects that put drivers and cargo at risk

- Persistent check-engine or warning lights linked to ongoing problems

- Major safety recalls that fail to resolve underlying defects

To qualify, the problem must occur during the warranty period and continue after a reasonable number of attempts to repair. Documenting every visit to the shop is very important, since strong records can make or break a claim under the California Lemon Law.

## California Lemon Law for Fleets

Fleet operators face unique challenges when even one vehicle goes down. For delivery services, transportation companies, or contractors with multiple trucks on the road, a single breakdown can ripple across the entire operation.

The California Lemon Law for fleets offers protection, but only under specific conditions.

## Number of Vehicles and Eligibility

The law draws a sharp line at five vehicles. Businesses with five or fewer registered in California may qualify for relief.

Once the fleet grows beyond that number, Lemon Law protections generally fall away. This restriction reflects the law’s purpose: shielding smaller businesses that lack the resources to absorb repeated losses.

## Special Considerations for Fleet Claims

Unlike personal claims, fleet issues often reveal themselves as patterns. If multiple vehicles experience the same defect, it can point to a manufacturing problem rather than bad luck.

Recognizing these trends early is a must. A qualified California Lemon Law attorney can review whether recurring defects across a fleet strengthen a claim.

## Documentation and Recordkeeping

Strong documentation is the backbone of any Lemon Law case, especially for fleets. Business owners should keep a full paper trail for each vehicle, including:

- Purchase or lease agreements

- Warranty details and service contracts

- Repair orders with dates and technician notes

- Vehicle downtime records

Many fleet operators now use digital management systems to track maintenance and repairs. These systems not only improve operations but also make it easier to demonstrate eligibility under the lemon law for fleets when defects pile up.

## Filing and Resolving a Lemon Law Claim

For business owners, the Lemon Law process can feel overwhelming. The good news is that claims follow a fairly clear path when you know what to expect.

Here are the main steps:

## 1. Gather Documentation

Keep a complete record for each vehicle. This should include:

- Purchase or lease agreements

- Warranty paperwork

- Repair orders and technician notes

- A timeline of repair attempts

- Dates when the vehicle was out of service

Thorough documentation is needed in California Lemon Law for commercial vehicle claims, especially if downtime causes lost productivity.

## 2. Consult a California Lemon Law Attorney

An experienced California Lemon Law attorney can confirm eligibility, review your records, and communicate directly with the manufacturer.

Attorneys who understand lemon law for fleets can also identify patterns across multiple vehicles that strengthen a case.

## 3. File the Claim

Once the claim is filed, the manufacturer has a limited time to respond.

The law requires prompt action, but if the response is delayed or unsatisfactory, your attorney can escalate the case through negotiation or litigation.

## 4. Resolution Options

The remedies for California lemon law claims include one of two ways:

- **Buyback**: The manufacturer repurchases the defective vehicle, minus a mileage offset from before the defect appeared.

- **Replacement**: The replacement vehicle is swapped for a substantially similar working model.

Compensation may also cover registration fees, taxes, and other direct expenses. However, business losses like missed income may not be included. This depends on case specific facts. Check with your attorney as these items add up and can be substantial.

## 5. Settlement vs. Litigation

Most Lemon Law cases end in settlement, with manufacturers agreeing to a buyback or replacement once the paperwork is clear. This route is faster and avoids unnecessary costs.

If a manufacturer disputes the claim - arguing the vehicle defect doesn’t qualify or wasn’t reported properly - litigation may be required. In that case, a California Lemon Law attorney can present records, repair history, and expert testimony to push for a fair outcome.

